\chapter{Tutorial: Enrich, verify, export}
\label{ch:tutorial-enrich-verify-export}

[[enrich-verify-export]] picks up where the first-search tutorial
(\cref{ch:tutorial-first-search}) leaves off: the learner has a session
of search hits and now has to turn it into something they can trust and
hand on.
The object of learning is that a session becomes more trustworthy in
\emph{stages}---metadata and open-access links are filled in, currency is
checked against Crossref, and the record is written out for a reader---and
that every stage has two faces: one for a person reading a terminal and
one for a program reading its output.

The arc keeps the session invariant and varies the stage:
\begin{enumerate}
\item seed a session (step~1, the same search as the earlier tutorials),
\item enrich it (step~2),
\item verify it, first for a person and then for a program (steps~3--4),
\item export it, first as a document and then as a fragment (steps~5--6),
\item sort the export formats by that distinction (step~7).
\end{enumerate}
Steps~2, 3 and~5 are variation theory's separation pattern: the same
session passes through three different commands, so the learner sees what
each stage adds without anything else changing.
Steps~3--4 and~5--6 are each a contrast pattern with a single varied
aspect---one flag in the first pair, one format in the second---which is
what makes the human/machine distinction visible rather than told.
The closing question is the fusion move: it asks the learner to apply the
document-versus-fragment distinction across all the formats at once.

\section{Tutorial source}

The whole tutorial is one tangled root chunk, with each step in its own
named sub-chunk so the prose can explain a step's role without breaking
the Markdown that pytorial reads.

<<[[enrich-verify-export.md]]>>=
---
id: enrich-verify-export
title: Enrich, verify, export
summary: Fill in metadata, check for retractions, and export a session for a reader or a program.
---

<<seed session step>>

<<enrich step>>

<<verify step>>

<<verify json step>>

<<export all step>>

<<export table step>>

<<fragment or document question step>>
@

\section{Step 1: Seed a session}

Every later step needs a session to act on, and the learner should create
it rather than find it pre-made: the search line is the one thing this
tutorial shares verbatim with the earlier ones, and repeating it turns the
[[-n]] flag from something once seen into something habitual.
The query, provider and limit are the same as in
\cref{ch:tutorial-first-search} on purpose---the hits are already cached,
so the step is fast, and nothing about the \emph{search} is new here.
Only the session name varies.

The anchor is Scholar's own confirmation line, which is printed
unconditionally when [[-n]] is given; the number of hits is deliberately
not part of the anchor, because provider ranking can change and the step
should not fail for a reason the learner cannot see.

<<seed session step>>=
# Seed a session to work on

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+search\s+.*-n\s+check-sources\b
  - "Saved session:"
hint: "The same search as before, saved under the session name check-sources with -n."
```

Run a small search and save it as a session named `check-sources`:

```
scholar search "literate programming" -p openalex -l 5 -n check-sources
```

The rest of this tutorial works on that session. Everything here happens
in a data directory private to the tutorial, so the session will not
appear in your own session list afterwards.
@

\section{Step 2: Enrich the session}

Search results are thin: a provider returns what it indexes, which may
lack an abstract, an open-access link or both.
[[scholar enrich]] looks each paper up by DOI in the other providers and
in Unpaywall, and it is the first stage where the learner sees the session
change without a new search.

Unpaywall requires a contact email, so the outcome of this step depends on
whether [[SCHOLAR_EMAIL]] is set in the learner's environment.
Both outcomes are correct behaviour and both are worth seeing: with the
email, Scholar prints an open-access tally; without it, Scholar prints a
hint naming the variable and skips only the open-access part.
The required pattern therefore accepts either line, and the instructions
say what each means, so a learner without the variable set is not told
they did something wrong.
The second anchor, the final [[Enriched]] count, is printed in both
cases.

<<enrich step>>=
# Fill in missing metadata

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+enrich\s+check-sources\b
  - mode: regex
    pattern: Open access:|SCHOLAR_EMAIL
  - Enriched
hint: "One verb fills in what the search left out; give it the session name."
```

Run `scholar enrich check-sources`.

Enrichment looks up each paper by DOI to fill in missing abstracts and
metadata, asks Unpaywall for open-access links, and checks Crossref for
retractions on the way.

Watch the line about open access:

- `Open access: N of M checked papers` means Unpaywall was consulted.
- `set SCHOLAR_EMAIL` means it was skipped: Unpaywall requires a contact
  email, and Scholar only sends requests when `SCHOLAR_EMAIL` is set.
  Everything else in this step still runs.

The last line, `Enriched N papers.`, counts papers that gained new
metadata. Zero is normal for well-indexed papers.
@

\section{Step 3: Verify currency}

Enrichment already checked Crossref for retractions as a side effect.
This step runs the same check as its own command, because the learner
should know the check exists independently of enrichment: a session
enriched months ago can be re-verified without touching its metadata.
The result is saved back to the session, which is why the report matters
for the exports later.

The anchors are the two words that appear in every report regardless of
what was found: the [[retracted]] count is printed even when it is zero,
and the [[ok]] count follows it.
Nothing in the anchor assumes the papers are clean.

<<verify step>>=
# Check for retractions and newer versions

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+verify\s+check-sources\b(?!.*--json)
  - retracted
  - mode: regex
    pattern: \d+ ok\b
hint: "Ask Scholar whether any paper in the session has been retracted, corrected or superseded."
```

Run `scholar verify check-sources`.

Scholar asks Crossref, for every paper with a DOI, whether it has been
retracted or corrected, or whether a newer version exists. The report
ends with one line of counts: retracted, corrected, superseded, preprint,
ok, unknown.

The status is saved into the session, so later exports can show it.
@

\section{Step 4: The same check, for a program}

This is the first contrast of the tutorial: the command of step~3 with
one flag added.
The learner has just read the human-facing report; now the same
information arrives as a JSON array, one object per paper, and nothing
else about the command changes.
Placing the two directly after each other is what lets the learner see
that the flag changes the \emph{face} of the check and not the check.

The anchor is the [[status]] field of one object.
It is quoted as it appears in the JSON, because that is exactly the
detail that differs from the table: a program looks for a key, a person
looks for a word.

<<verify json step>>=
# The same check, for a program

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+verify\s+check-sources\s+--json\b
  - '"status": "ok"'
hint: "Same command as before, plus the flag that switches the report to JSON."
```

Run `scholar verify check-sources --json`.

The check is identical to the previous step, but the report is now a
JSON array with one object per paper (`doi`, `title`, `status`, and the
details behind the status). This is the form a script or an agent reads
when it decides what to do with each paper; the table you saw before is
the form a person reads.
@

\section{Step 5: Export the session as documents}

Exporting is the stage where the session leaves Scholar.
The default format, [[all]], writes three files at once: a CSV for
spreadsheets and scripts, a LaTeX report, and the BibTeX file the report
cites.
The learner lists the directory afterwards so the three files are seen as
a set, and then looks at the first line of the report, which is
[[\documentclass]]: the report is a complete document that compiles on its
own.
That first line is the set-up for the next step's contrast, so the
instructions point at it explicitly.

The anchors are Scholar's confirmation and two of the three file names,
which are derived from the [[-o]] base name and therefore stable.

<<export all step>>=
# Export the session as documents

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+export\s+check-sources\s+.*-o\s+check-sources\b
  - "Exported:"
  - check-sources.csv
  - check-sources.tex
hint: "Export with -f all and the base name check-sources, then list the directory."
```

Export the session and look at what was written:

```
scholar sessions export check-sources -f all -o check-sources
ls
head -1 check-sources.tex
```

`-f all` writes a CSV (one row per paper, with the decision, tags and the
verification status), a LaTeX report, and the BibTeX file the report
cites. The first line of `check-sources.tex` is `\documentclass`: the
report is a complete document you can compile as it is.
@

\section{Step 6: Export the session as a fragment}

The audit table is the export a paper's appendix wants: one row per work
with the reason it was kept or excluded.
It differs from the report of step~5 in exactly one way that the learner
can see at the top of the file---it is not a document but a fragment,
with a comment header telling the includer which packages the preamble
must load.
Showing the two heads one after the other is the contrast that carries the
step: same session, same command family, one format switch, and the
output goes from something you compile to something you [[\input]].

The anchors are the fragment header Scholar writes and the table
environment, both of which are fixed text.

<<export table step>>=
# Export an audit table for a paper

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+export\s+check-sources\s+.*-f\s+table\b
  - scholar LaTeX fragment
  - longtable
hint: "The table format takes --lang and --label; write it to the base name check-table and look at its head."
```

Now write the audit table and look at its head:

```
scholar sessions export check-sources -f table --lang en --label check -o check-table
head check-table.tex
```

The audit table lists one row per work with the reason it was kept or
excluded, ready for a paper's appendix. Compare its first lines with the
report from the previous step: this file is a *fragment*, meant to be
`\input` into a document of yours, and its comment header names the
packages that document's preamble must load. The table is always a
fragment; the report is a document by default and becomes a fragment
only with `--no-standalone`.
@

\section{Step 7: Sort the formats}

The closing question makes the learner apply the distinction of the last
two steps to every LaTeX-producing form of the command, including one
they did not run.
[[latex]] by default is the compilable document they saw in step~5;
[[table]] is the fragment they saw in step~6; [[latex --no-standalone]]
was only mentioned, and it is the option that tests whether the learner
understood the rule rather than memorised the two examples.
[[csv]] is the distractor: it is neither, and a learner who picks it has
confused \enquote{not a document} with \enquote{a fragment}.

<<fragment or document question step>>=
# Which exports are fragments?

```tutorial-step
kind: multi_select
options:
  - latex
  - latex --no-standalone
  - table
  - csv
answers:
  - latex --no-standalone
  - table
hint: "A fragment is LaTeX meant to be \\input into your own document; a document compiles on its own; a CSV is neither."
```

Choose every export format that produces a LaTeX *fragment* meant to be
`\input` into a document of your own, rather than a document that
compiles on its own.
@
